Entity: visual acuity was 20/400 OD and 20/50 OS | Category: Treatment
Context: This is a 16-year-old Caucasian male with a 2-week history of decreasing visual acuity. Past medical history was unremarkable, although he did report non-specific recent upper respiratory tract infection. Uveitis survey and laboratory evaluation were unremarkable. At time of ocular examination, visual acuity was 20/400 OD and 20/50 OS. Intraocular pressure was 15 mmHg OU. Dilated fundus examination of the right eye found multifocal placoid hypopigmented lesions at the RPE. Examination of the left fundus showed a smaller number of lesions (Figure 
). Fundus photography and fluorescein angiography demonstrated multifocal and diffuse lesions scattered throughout the posterior pole and peripheral retina of both eyes.
Treatment plan was 30 mg prednisone taper, and continuation of oral ciprofloxacin BID started previously by PCP for upper respiratory tract infection. Follow-up examinations took place at the interval of 2, 4, 6, 8, and 12 weeks, and 6 months, and 1-year duration. The patient was then followed yearly for a total of 4 years. The lesions resolved over a 4-week period with improved visual acuities to 20/60 OD and 20/20 OS by 12 weeks and 20/40 OD and 20/20 OS by 6 months (Figure 
). There have been no recurrences. HLA testing was performed, and HLA-A3 and HLA-C7 were present.

Is this a valid, specific clinical entity of the stated category (not demographic, not vague, not hallucinated)?

